{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Footer
  Phase 10. Lives in the footer section group, so it is on every page.

  Sources of truth:
  - PHASE-1-WEBSITE-AUDIT.md §16 (the footer audit) for what is missing and why
    it matters; §29.1 (recommended tree) places sections/footer.liquid and
    sections/footer-group.json; §29.2 binds both group sections with enabled_on;
    §29.7 gives this section's recommended settings and its two block types.
  - PHASE-2-DESIGN-SYSTEM.md §29.1 (footer belongs to the group, not to a
    template, and therefore carries no presets), §29.6 (this section's setting
    list and the one-colour-setting rule), §29.7 (link_list block, max 4),
    §18.1 (the icon set and the social rule), §18.2 (--icon-lg is the footer
    social size and nothing else).

  WHAT PHASE 1 FOUND, AND WHAT THIS ANSWERS.
  §16: "two taglines, one logo, two dead links. No copyright, no policy links,
  no contact, no newsletter, no navigation, no payment icons." The <footer>
  element itself was a correct landmark and is kept as one. FOOT-01 asks for
  navigation and a legal row, and records the wayfinding reason: below 900px the
  prototype's header scrolled away with nothing to bring it back on a 4,382px
  phone page, and the footer is where the menu reappears. FOOT-02 records the
  two social links as href="#" with no accounts confirmed.

  THE COPY IS THE PROTOTYPE'S OWN COPY, NOT NEW COPY.
  The tagline and strapline defaults below are "Different People. Same Purpose."
  (prototype line 156) and "A Brighter Tomorrow" (line 164), which Phase 1 §16
  asks to preserve as the footer's brand band and Phase 2 §29.6 names as this
  section's two content settings. Both are Theme Editor fields. BRAND-08 is open
  — the project has four competing taglines and no canonical set — so nothing
  here adds a fifth, and nothing invents a claim, a statistic or a promise.

  SHOPIFY IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR EVERY DESTINATION.
  Menu items come from a link list the merchant builds in Navigation. Policy
  links come from shop.policies, so a merchant never retypes a URL the platform
  already owns and a policy that is unpublished simply does not appear. The
  copyright year comes from 'now'. Not one href in this file is written by hand,
  which is the point of NAV-03 (six href="#" anchors in the prototype).

  THE SOCIAL URLS ARE THEME SETTINGS, NOT SECTION SETTINGS.
  Phase 2 §29.6 specifies show_social as the section's switch and says it
  "renders the theme-level URLs, never its own", and §28.4's level test agrees:
  a brand's Instagram address is one store-wide fact, and two different values
  on two pages would be a bug rather than a choice. They are read from
  settings.* below. Until config/settings_schema.json carries them the values
  are blank, the row does not render, and the Theme Editor says why.

  THE SOCIAL MARKS ARE WORDS, NOT ICONS, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
  Phase 1 §29.1 lists snippets/icon-facebook.liquid and icon-instagram.liquid as
  recommended; neither has been drawn, and this phase does not draw them. The
  raster files the prototype used (images/icon-facebook.png, icon-instagram.png,
  130x130 RGBA) are not in assets/ and Phase 1 classifies both as "replace with
  SVG snippet" — colour is baked into their pixels, so they cannot follow
  --accent-current, a hover or a light surface. A word is a real accessible name
  at every surface and needs no asset. When the marks are drawn, --icon-lg 28px
  is the size Phase 2 §18.2 reserves for exactly this row.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE.
  - A newsletter block. Out of scope for Phase 10; Phase 1 §29.7 marks it ADD
    LATER and Phase 2 §29.1 reserves a second group section for it.
  - (RESOLVED IN PHASE 11.) The footer wordmark was blocked because the only
    logo in the theme was sections/header.liquid's own image_picker, and a
    section cannot read another section's settings — so shipping it would have
    meant a second upload of one asset. Phase 11 promoted the logo to
    settings.logo, and the show_logo setting above now feeds on it.
    Phase 3 still records VECTOR LOGO REQUIRED: the current wordmark renders at
    about half its intended size because of its transparent padding.
    (The earlier note cited INV-04 for the duplication. That was wrong: INV-04
    is the asset's raster quality and URL-unsafe name; the duplicate-file id is
    DUP-04, and neither actually described a second upload field.)
  - Payment icons, and a country/currency selector. Both are real gaps in §16
    and neither needs any business information — shop.enabled_payment_types and
    a localization form would do it — but both are new surfaces rather than the
    missing footer, and Phase 10 is completing what exists.
  - copyright_text, which Phase 1 §29.7 offers as a free-text field marked
    BUSINESS INFORMATION REQUIRED. A typed year goes stale the moment it is
    typed; this derives it. There is no show_copyright either: a contentinfo
    landmark without a copyright line is the defect being fixed.
  - spacing_top / spacing_bottom / anchor_id. This is page chrome bound to a
    group, not a band in the section rhythm, so it takes the page padding
    directly the way sections/main-cart.liquid does. Phase 2 §29.6 allows the
    footer six settings; this uses five.

  ZERO JavaScript.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign tagline = section.settings.tagline | strip
  assign description = section.settings.description | strip
  assign strapline = section.settings.strapline | strip

  assign surface_class = 'surface-dark'
  if section.settings.surface == 'light'
    assign surface_class = 'surface-light'
  endif

  comment
    A policy object is blank until the merchant publishes it, which is what
    Shopify's own footer pattern tests. The loop stops at the first published
    one, because all this decides is whether the row exists at all.
  endcomment
  assign has_policies = false
  for policy in shop.policies
    if policy != blank
      assign has_policies = true
      break
    endif
  endfor
  assign show_policies = false
  if section.settings.show_policies and has_policies
    assign show_policies = true
  endif

  assign facebook_url = settings.social_facebook_url
  assign instagram_url = settings.social_instagram_url
  assign tiktok_url = settings.social_tiktok_url
  assign youtube_url = settings.social_youtube_url
  assign show_social = false
  if section.settings.show_social
    if facebook_url != blank or instagram_url != blank or tiktok_url != blank or youtube_url != blank
      assign show_social = true
    endif
  endif

  comment
    The utility row is the policy links and nothing else now. The social marks
    moved up into the brand row, so counting them here would render an empty
    .footer__utility on a store that has social addresses but no published
    policies.
  endcomment
  assign show_utility = show_policies

  comment
    'now' is Liquid's own input to the date filter and is evaluated when the
    page renders. The alternative Phase 1 §29.7 offers — copyright_text, a free
    text field — is stale the moment it is typed, and nobody edits a footer in
    January.
  endcomment
  assign year = 'now' | date: '%Y'

  assign classes = 'footer '
  assign classes = classes | append: surface_class
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  No render guard. Every other content section in this theme renders nothing
  until it is configured; this one always renders, because the thing Phase 1 §16
  records as missing is the contentinfo landmark and the copyright line, and
  both exist the moment the section does. The stylesheet link therefore sits at
  the top of the file, unwrapped, the way sections/main-cart.liquid's does.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'section-footer.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- comment -%}
  The landmark is written here rather than delegated to the schema's tag, and
  the schema's tag is "div" for a reason that is not cosmetic: <footer> maps to
  the contentinfo role only while it is not a descendant of article, aside,
  main, nav or section. Setting the tag to "section" would wrap this element in
  sectioning content and silently demote the only contentinfo on the site to a
  generic group. A div wrapper does not, so the role survives; it is written out
  as well so the file states its own contract. This is the same choice
  sections/header.liquid makes for the banner landmark.
{%- endcomment -%}
<footer class="{{ classes }}" role="contentinfo">
  <div class="footer__inner container">
    {%- comment -%}
      Phase 11. The footer wordmark, which Phase 10 recorded as blocked: "a
      section cannot read another section's settings, so shipping this would
      mean a second upload of one asset... Promoting the logo to a theme setting
      is the fix." Phase 11 promoted it, so one upload now serves both, and
      --logo-height-footer has existed in the token file since Phase 2.

      Rendered only when the merchant asks for it AND a logo exists: a checkbox
      that produces an empty gap is worse than no checkbox. The link goes home,
      matching the header's, and the alt text is empty because the adjacent
      tagline already names the brand — an image whose only job is decorative
      repetition should not be announced twice.

      PHASE 16: THE IMAGE IS DECORATIVE, THE LINK STILL HAS TO BE NAMED.
      alt="" on the only content of an anchor leaves the anchor with no
      accessible name at all — a screen reader announces "link" and nothing
      else, which is SC 2.4.4 and SC 4.1.2 at Level A. The tagline that names
      the brand is a sibling of this anchor, not inside it, so it never named
      the link. The hidden span below is the name; the image stays decorative,
      which is what the paragraph above is actually asking for.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      THE BRAND ROW. Logo, tagline, social marks and closing line on one line,
      which is the arrangement the approved footer design shows. They used to sit
      in three separate rows -- logo and tagline here, social inside
      .footer__utility beside the policy links, the closing line down in
      .footer__bottom beside the copyright -- and no amount of CSS could pull
      them together, because they were in different parents. The policy links and
      the copyright stay where they were.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="footer__brand">
    {%- if section.settings.show_logo -%}
      <a class="footer__logo-link" href="{{ routes.root_url }}">
        {%- if settings.logo != blank -%}
          {{
            settings.logo
            | image_url: width: 336
            | image_tag:
              class: 'footer__logo',
              alt: '',
              widths: '112, 168, 224, 336',
              sizes: '112px',
              loading: 'lazy'
          }}
        {%- else -%}
          {% render 'image-fallback',
            file: 'logo.png',
            class: 'footer__logo',
            sizes: '112px',
            width: 500,
            height: 500
          %}
        {%- endif -%}
        <span class="visually-hidden">{{ shop.name }}</span>
      </a>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if tagline != blank or description != blank -%}
      <div class="footer__lockup-text">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if tagline != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The break is the merchant's. The design sets this on two lines and the
        setting is one string, so where it wraps was whatever the column width
        happened to allow -- three lines at 1440. newline_to_br puts it back
        under their control, the same way the hero and the verse quote work.
        escape runs FIRST: reversed, it would eat the <br> it just made. The
        escape is not optional either -- Liquid does not auto-escape, so a
        merchant setting printed raw is a stored-XSS hole.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="footer__tagline">{{ tagline | escape | newline_to_br }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if description != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        A short line under the tagline. The tagline is a slogan; this is the
        sentence that says what the brand is, for a reader who has just arrived
        at the bottom of a page. It ships EMPTY: brand copy is the owner's, and
        a description invented here would be a claim the theme made up.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="footer__description">{{ description | escape }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if tagline != blank or description != blank -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

      {%- if show_social -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            No target="_blank". Opening a new window is a change of context a
            link gives no warning of, and it is not the theme's decision to make
            on the customer's behalf; with the link left alone, a person who
            wants a new tab still gets one from their own modifier click.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <nav class="footer__social" aria-label="{{ 'footer.social_label' | t }}">
            {%- comment -%}
              THE MARKS ARE NOW DRAWN, AND THE NAME DID NOT GO WITH THE WORD.

              This row used to render the platform's name as visible text, on
              the reasoning recorded above: a word is a real accessible name at
              every surface and needs no asset, and the SVGs had not been drawn.
              They have been now, so the visible word becomes a mark — but the
              word itself stays, in .visually-hidden, because that is where the
              link's accessible name was coming from. Deleting it would leave
              four links named nothing, which is the usual way an icon row
              regresses. Same construction as the header's icon-only controls.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <ul class="footer__social-list" role="list">
              {%- if instagram_url != blank -%}
                <li>
                  <a class="footer__link footer__social-link" href="{{ instagram_url }}">
                    {% render 'icon-instagram' %}
                    <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'footer.social.instagram' | t }}</span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              {%- endif -%}
              {%- if facebook_url != blank -%}
                <li>
                  <a class="footer__link footer__social-link" href="{{ facebook_url }}">
                    {% render 'icon-facebook' %}
                    <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'footer.social.facebook' | t }}</span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              {%- endif -%}
              {%- if tiktok_url != blank -%}
                <li>
                  <a class="footer__link footer__social-link" href="{{ tiktok_url }}">
                    {% render 'icon-tiktok' %}
                    <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'footer.social.tiktok' | t }}</span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              {%- endif -%}
              {%- if youtube_url != blank -%}
                <li>
                  <a class="footer__link footer__social-link" href="{{ youtube_url }}">
                    {% render 'icon-youtube' %}
                    <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'footer.social.youtube' | t }}</span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              {%- endif -%}
            </ul>
          </nav>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if strapline != blank -%}
        <p class="footer__strapline">{{ strapline }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- if section.blocks.size > 0 -%}
      <div class="footer__blocks">
        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          {%- case block.type -%}
            {%- when 'link_list' -%}
              {%- liquid
                assign menu_object = linklists[block.settings.menu]
                assign column_heading = block.settings.heading | strip
                comment
                  A column with no heading is a list of links with nothing
                  naming it, and its nav landmark would be unlabelled. Rather
                  than invent a word for it, the fall-back is the merchant's own
                  name for the menu, set in Navigation — which is the only place
                  in the store that already knows what these links have in
                  common.
                endcomment
                if column_heading == blank
                  assign column_heading = menu_object.title
                endif
              -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                The test is the rendered outcome, not the setting: a merchant
                can pick a menu here and later empty it in Navigation, and a
                heading standing over no links is the same defect Phase 6
                avoids by testing has_products rather than "is a collection
                chosen".
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- if menu_object != blank and menu_object.links.size > 0 -%}
                <nav
                  class="footer__column"
                  aria-labelledby="FooterMenu-{{ block.id }}"
                  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
                >
                  {%- comment -%}
                    An h2. The page's h1 belongs to the hero on the home page and
                    to the main-* section on a template, and nothing in the
                    footer sits under a clearable heading, so unlike the Phase 6
                    and Phase 7 bands this level is fixed rather than derived.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <h2 class="footer__heading" id="FooterMenu-{{ block.id }}">{{ column_heading }}</h2>
                  <ul class="footer__menu" role="list">
                    {%- for link in menu_object.links -%}
                      <li>
                        <a
                          class="footer__link{% if link.active %} is-active{% endif %}"
                          href="{{ link.url }}"
                          {% if link.active %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
                        >{{ link.title }}</a>
                      </li>
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  </ul>
                </nav>
              {%- elsif request.design_mode -%}
                <p class="footer__notice" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
                  {{ 'sections.footer.no_menu' | t }}
                </p>
              {%- endif -%}

            {%- when 'text' -%}
              {%- if block.settings.heading != blank or block.settings.body != blank -%}
                <div class="footer__column" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
                  {%- if block.settings.heading != blank -%}
                    <h2 class="footer__heading">{{ block.settings.heading }}</h2>
                  {%- endif -%}
                  {%- if block.settings.body != blank -%}
                    <div class="footer__text">{{ block.settings.body }}</div>
                  {%- endif -%}
                </div>
              {%- endif -%}
          {%- endcase -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if show_utility -%}
      <div class="footer__utility">
        {%- if show_policies -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The titles and the URLs are Shopify's, in the store's own language.
            A merchant who has not published a policy gets no link to it rather
            than a link to an empty page, which is why the test is per policy
            and not "does this shop have policies".
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <nav class="footer__policies" aria-label="{{ 'footer.policies_label' | t }}">
            <ul class="footer__policy-list" role="list">
              {%- for policy in shop.policies -%}
                {%- if policy != blank -%}
                  <li>
                    <a class="footer__link" href="{{ policy.url }}">{{ policy.title }}</a>
                  </li>
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </ul>
          </nav>
        {%- endif -%}

      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="footer__bottom">
      <p class="footer__copyright">
        {{ 'footer.copyright' | t: year: year, name: shop.name }}
      </p>
    </div>

    {%- if request.design_mode -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign notices = ''
        if section.settings.show_policies and has_policies == false
          assign notices = notices | append: 'no_policies|'
        endif
        if section.settings.show_social and show_social == false
          assign notices = notices | append: 'no_social|'
        endif
      -%}
      {%- if notices != blank -%}
        <div class="footer__notices">
          {%- if notices contains 'no_policies' -%}
            <p class="footer__notice">{{ 'sections.footer.no_policies' | t }}</p>
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if notices contains 'no_social' -%}
            <p class="footer__notice">{{ 'sections.footer.no_social' | t }}</p>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</footer>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Footer",
  "tag": "div",
  "class": "footer-section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Brand band"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_logo",
      "label": "Show the logo",
      "default": false,
      "info": "Uses the logo from Theme settings > Brand. Nothing is shown until one is uploaded."
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "tagline",
      "label": "Tagline",
      "default": "Different People. Same Purpose.",
      "info": "A short line above the footer columns. It is not a heading and nothing else depends on it, so clearing it simply removes the line."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "description",
      "label": "Short description",
      "info": "Optional. One line under the tagline. Left empty it is not rendered."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "strapline",
      "label": "Closing line",
      "default": "A Brighter Tomorrow",
      "info": "Sits beside the copyright on the bottom row."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Store policies"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_policies",
      "label": "Show links to your store policies",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Refund, privacy, terms, shipping and contact information come straight from Settings > Policies in Shopify admin. Nothing is retyped here, and a policy you have not published is not linked."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Social"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The addresses live in Theme settings, because they are the same on every page. Add only the accounts you actually operate — each icon appears once its address is filled in, and none is shown otherwise."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_social",
      "label": "Show the social links",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Layout"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "surface",
      "label": "Colour scheme",
      "info": "Ink is the approved treatment. The footer carries a hairline along its top edge so it still reads as its own band when the section above it is ink as well.",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "dark",
          "label": "Ink"
        },
        {
          "value": "light",
          "label": "Cream"
        }
      ],
      "default": "dark"
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "link_list",
      "name": "Menu",
      "limit": 4,
      "settings": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "heading",
          "label": "Heading",
          "info": "Leave this empty and the menu's own name from Navigation is used, so the column is never unlabelled."
        },
        {
          "type": "link_list",
          "id": "menu",
          "label": "Menu",
          "info": "Build the menu in Navigation, then choose it here. The theme ships no footer menu of its own, and a column with no links does not render on the live store."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "name": "Text",
      "limit": 2,
      "settings": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "heading",
          "label": "Heading"
        },
        {
          "type": "richtext",
          "id": "body",
          "label": "Text",
          "info": "Contact details belong here. The theme supplies none and guesses nothing — add the address, email or phone number you want customers to use."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "enabled_on": {
    "groups": [
      "footer"
    ]
  }
}
{% endschema %}
